🚨 MorniaLabs — Arduino Police LED Simulator 🔴🔵⚪

Welcome to MorniaLabs! 👋

This project demonstrates how to create a police-style flashing LED light effect using an Arduino Uno and three LEDs. 🚔⚡

With a combination of different flashing speeds, alternating colors, and synchronized light patterns, this project recreates a fun emergency-vehicle-inspired lighting effect using simple electronics and Arduino code.

⚠️ Disclaimer: This is an educational electronics project for hobby and simulation purposes only. It does not reproduce an actual emergency vehicle system.
✨ Features
🔴 Red LED — Connected to digital pin 7.
🔵 Blue LED — Connected to digital pin 4.
⚪ White LED — Connected to digital pin 2.
⚡ Fast flashing sequences.
🔄 Alternating red and blue light patterns.
🚨 Synchronized LED flashing effects.
🛠️ Beginner-friendly Arduino code.
💡 Simple and affordable electronic components.
🎓 Great for learning basic Arduino programming and digital outputs.
🧰 Components Required

To build this project, you will need:

Component	Quantity
Arduino Uno	1
Red LED 🔴	1
Blue LED 🔵	1
White LED ⚪	1
220Ω–330Ω resistors	3
Breadboard	1
Jumper wires	As needed
USB cable	1

💡 Important: Use a current-limiting resistor for each LED to protect the LEDs and your Arduino.

🔌 Wiring Diagram

Connect the LEDs to your Arduino Uno as follows:

LED Color	Arduino Pin	Connection
🔴 Red	Digital Pin 7	Pin 7 → Resistor → LED anode (+)
🔵 Blue	Digital Pin 4	Pin 4 → Resistor → LED anode (+)
⚪ White	Digital Pin 2	Pin 2 → Resistor → LED anode (+)
All LEDs	GND	LED cathodes (−) → GND

Make sure each LED is connected with the correct polarity.

💻 Software Requirements
🖥️ Arduino IDE
🔌 Arduino Uno board
📦 No additional libraries required!

This project uses standard Arduino functions such as pinMode(), digitalWrite(), delay(), and for() loops.

🚀 Getting Started

Follow these steps to run the project:

📥 Clone or download this repository.
🔌 Connect your Arduino Uno to your computer using a USB data cable.
💻 Open the Arduino IDE.
📂 Open the project's .ino file.
⚙️ Select Arduino Uno under Tools → Board.
🔗 Select the correct port under Tools → Port.
⬆️ Click Upload.
🎉 Watch your red, blue, and white LEDs flash!
⚙️ How It Works

The program runs a repeating sequence of LED patterns:

🔴 Red Flash Sequence: The red LED flashes rapidly four times.
🔵 Blue Flash Sequence: The blue LED flashes rapidly four times.
⚪ White Flash: The white LED flashes briefly.
🚨 Red & Blue Synchronization: Both LEDs flash together several times.
🔴⚪ Red & White Combination: The red and white LEDs turn on together.
🔵⚪ Blue & White Combination: The blue and white LEDs turn on together.
🔄 Repeat: The entire sequence starts again automatically.

The delay() function controls the timing of each flash. For example, delay(80) pauses the program for approximately 80 milliseconds.

🎯 Project Goals

The goal of this project is to explore fundamental electronics and programming concepts, including:

Understanding Arduino digital output pins.
Controlling LEDs using code.
Learning timing and delay functions.
Using loops to repeat actions.
Creating custom lighting sequences.
Combining multiple outputs into one project.

Whether you're just getting started with Arduino or experimenting with your own electronics projects, this is a fun way to learn! 🧑‍💻⚡

